    "sourceSha256": "e2136004fda282c399554481defd63f89a2275180a1e625095c88c6b9a232bea",
    "note": "Human hemoglobin from one study, both at 1.25 Angstrom by X-ray diffraction: deoxy (T state, entry 2DN2) and oxy (R state, entry 2DN1). The coordinates are the prepared composite hemoglobin-tr-motion, built from its recipe: ten chains: T and R copies of the four subunits, and a second copy (I and J, backbone only) of the T alpha 2 beta 2 pair that stays at its starting position as a faded reference. Every atom is a deposited atom of 2DN2 or 2DN1 moved by one rigid-body transform. The whole composite is turned once so that the measured screw axis of alpha 2 beta 2 points out of the opening view; within it the 2DN2 chains keep their deposited arrangement, the 2DN1 dimer and its assembly-operator copy are fitted on the T alpha 1 beta 1 pair, and the intermediate positions are fractions of the measured screw motion of the T alpha 2 beta 2 pair (14.1 degrees about one axis, 1.3 Angstrom along it). Waters and the two toluene molecules of 2DN1 are left out. The path between the two deposited structures is illustrative; only the T and R end points are measured."
  },
  "presentation": {
    "captionCard": "shown",
    "end": "hold"
  },
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "switch",
      "title": "Hemoglobin switches from T to R",
      "caption": "Green: alpha 1 beta 1, which stays fixed. Blue: alpha 2 beta 2, which turns 14.1 degrees about one axis. In the opening view you look straight down that axis. Once the turn starts, a faded copy marks where the pair began. Beta is the lighter shade in both pairs. When the turn ends, the T subunits give way to the deposited R subunits, with O2 on every heme. Then the R subunits give way to T again, without O2, and the pair turns back to its start, so the clip repeats. The path between the two deposited structures, in either direction, is illustrative.",
      "durationSeconds": 14,
